 "method": "Where approval levels add delay without changing decisions. For every level of the delegation of authority (dd_parameter doa) the engine reads each past file's approval steps (gov_approval_step): days at the level, and what the level did, both when it only passed the file up (pass-through) and when it was the deciding level. A level \"changes\" a file when it amends terms, declines or overturns the recommendation. Rates carry 95% Wilson intervals.  Redesign rules (deterministic):   1. Pass-through sign-off: a level whose pass-through change rate has an upper bound below 5% (n >= 30) and that      holds files for 2 days or more stops signing files it does not decide (it is copied, not asked).   2. Delegation lift: for each deciding level, files rated BB- or better that it decided with no change (n >= 30,      change rate upper bound below 8%): the level below takes them up to the 75th percentile of their limits,      rounded to SAR 5 million. The control given up is counted: files in that band where the level did change something. Days saved are medians per file times files per year over the history.",
 "data_note": "Decision history is a fictional seed published to Data Domains (domain governance, gov_* relations, ops/r13_seed_governance.py); outcomes (stage, DPD, default, rating drift) are the corporate book's own."
}